  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA deepening low over the central US will lift into the Great \nLakes tonight with a warm front moving into the Northeast. \nClouds will begin to increase tonight with a strong low level \njet developing. Stable air in the low levels will prevent most \nof the winds from mixing down but downslope areas of the Finger \nLakes into the foot hills south of the NY Thruway may see some \ngusts of 30 mph tonight. Showers and even a few thunderstorms \ndevelop tonight as the warm front lifts in so some thunder was \nadded to the grids after Midnight. \n\nThe region will be in the warm sector of the low tomorrow. \nTrends have brought the path of the low a little farther north \nso odds of some clearing for the Southern Tier and southward has\nincreased. This has helped increase the potential for a little \nbit of CAPE to develop (roughly between 200 and 400 J/kg). Given\nshear is over 50 knots, and the cold front being a strong \ntrigger for storms, the potential for thunderstorms along the \nfront to produce strong winds have increased. SPC has moved a \nslight risk into most of NEPA and parts of the Southern Tier. \nForecast hodographs do have some low level curvature ahead of \nthe front, so if there is enough sun to get better surface based\nCAPE (>500 J/kg), a brief spin up is possible. \n\nPrecipitation transitions to lake effect rain showers once the \nfront goes through tomorrow evening. NEPA will dry out quickly \nbehind the front as the lake effect rain showers will struggle \nto make it much past the Southern Tier of NY. 850 mb \ntemperatures plummet post front, falling to near -10C by \nSaturday morning. Forecast soundings really dry out the boundary\nlayer and a subsidence inversion lowers quickly but given the \nlake temperatures, lake effect showers will continue longer than\nmodeled. It is looking likely that some of the higher \nelevations of NY for the Southern Tier and northward could see \nsome flurries Saturday morning when the coldest air moves \nthrough.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nNext week the northeast looks to remain mostly under a large \nupper level ridge. With weak warm air advection aloft, 850 mb \ntemperatures warm to between 8C and 14C for much of the week. \nThis is well above average for mid October and with dry air in \nplace, sunny conditions will help day time temperatures warm \ninto the 60s and low 70s. Overnight lows will still be on the \ncooler side with good conditions for radiational cooling most \nnights.",
